    {
        "user": "U02E4DAQGSZ",
        "type": "message",
        "ts": "1738451063.923949",
        "client_msg_id": "9eaf523c-48da-426d-805b-04ce4082dda9",
        "text": "I tried making one once and yeah, its really hard mapping the polar look to mouse. I just did the up or right for +ve and it just seems wrong when its at certain angles. If you do a drag it can be really sensitive to the singularity that exists near the center which is where you naturally start a drag  so its pretty interesting you are removing that singularity with a local center of rotation. How are you tracking that?\n<https://observablehq.com/@tomlarkworthy/audio-inputs#cell-906>",
        "team": "T5TCAFTA9",
        "thread_ts": "1738449335.894129",
        "parent_user_id": "UC2A2ARPT",
        "blocks": [
            {
                "type": "rich_text",
                "block_id": "FThsc",
                "elements": [
                    {
                        "type": "rich_text_section",
                        "elements": [
                            {
                                "type": "text",
                                "text": "I tried making one once and yeah, its really hard mapping the polar look to mouse. I just did the up or right for +ve and it just seems wrong when its at certain angles. If you do a drag it can be really sensitive to the singularity that exists near the center which is where you naturally start a drag  so its pretty interesting you are removing that singularity with a local center of rotation. How are you tracking that?\n"
                            },
                            {
                                "type": "link",
                                "url": "https://observablehq.com/@tomlarkworthy/audio-inputs#cell-906"
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ]
            }
        ]
    },
    {
        "user": "UC2A2ARPT",
        "type": "message",
        "ts": "1738451474.359569",
        "edited": {
            "user": "UC2A2ARPT",
            "ts": "1738453804.000000"
        },
        "client_msg_id": "82DD40DC-A456-4019-8377-8A9D08F92108",
        "text": "I remember the path that the mouse has moved, take a bounding box of that path, take the center of the box, and use that as the local center of rotation. I also discard older segments of the path, keeping roughly 4pi*r of them, since that's enough to figure out roughly where the local center ought to be.\n\nThe logic for switching between modes on the fly (horizontal, vertical, or angular) is a bit dodgy. That's something that could be better, but there are also unavoidable edge cases \u2014 like when dragging rightward at the bottom of the circle (which could mean \"increase the value\" when treating the input as horizontal or \"decrease the value\" when treating it as angular), so my current approach is just to pick whichever one seems most likely and kinda lock-in on it after a handful of samples.",
        "team": "T5TCAFTA9",
        "thread_ts": "1738449335.894129",
        "parent_user_id": "UC2A2ARPT",
        "blocks": [
            {
                "type": "rich_text",
                "block_id": "uqXAQ",
                "elements": [
                    {
                        "type": "rich_text_section",
                        "elements": [
                            {
                                "type": "text",
                                "text": "I remember the path that the mouse has moved, take a bounding box of that path, take the center of the box, and use that as the local center of rotation. I also discard older segments of the path, keeping roughly 4pi*r of them, since that's enough to figure out roughly where the local center ought to be.\n\nThe logic for switching between modes on the fly (horizontal, vertical, or angular) is a bit dodgy"
                            },
                            {
                                "type": "text",
                                "text": ". T"
                            },
                            {
                                "type": "text",
                                "text": "hat's something that could be better, but there are also unavoidable edge cases"
                            },
                            {
                                "type": "text",
                                "text": " \u2014"
                            },
                            {
                                "type": "text",
                                "text": " like when dragging rightward at the bottom of the circle (which could mean \"increase the value\" when treating the input as horizontal or \"decrease the value\" when treating it as angular), so my current approach is just to pick whichever one seems most likely and kinda lock-in on it after a handful of samples."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ]
            }
        ],
